body:
> This seems like an unusual limitation for a game engine. I can see it currently doesn't work reliably, hence this PR. But is it really intended?

Note that even with your fix, the change is not fully atomic. While it does significantly decrease the chance of crash, by  removing the timeframe where `_ptr` is `nullptr`, there is still a chance of simultaneous read-write on `_ptr`. So, there's still chance of a crash.

The only way to completely fix the problem would be to use a mutex, which would be a substantial kneecap to our performance.

> Populating buffers on a background thread swapping them and reading on the rendering thread is standard practice for games.
> 
> How else are existing APIs like `RenderingServerDefault`'s...
> 
> ```c++
> 	//these go through command queue if they are in another thread
> 	FUNC3(texture_2d_update, RID, const Ref<Image> &, int)
> 	FUNC2(texture_3d_update, RID, const Vector<Ref<Image>> &)
> 	FUNC4(texture_external_update, RID, int, int, uint64_t)
> 	FUNC2(texture_proxy_update, RID, RID)
> ```
> 
> .. supposed to be consumed?
> 
> They're documented as going through the command queue. That's why they're implemented via those macros. How would a background thread _ever_ safely update an `Image` using these APIs? You _cannot_ safely hang onto a `Mutex` in the presence of these command buffers. Our else you'll run into more deadlocks like this one I fixed over #110353

It would be expected that the thread with write ownership makes a copy, and passes this copy to the command queue.
This is thread safe, because separate CoW copy instances of CowData can simultaneously read from the buffer, and try writing to it. If no threads write to it, the overhead is minimal. I'm not super familiar with `CommandQueueMT`, but I think this should already happen when a `Vector` is passed into `push`.

All this being said, I don't want to argue that there's no value in this pull request. While I don't think it's the right solution for the problem, you're right that it _would_ make `CowData` more resistant to crashes by misuse.

I've constructed a diff that achieves the same as your PR (I think), and we'll discuss it at the next core meeting.
Regardless of the outcome of the discussion, I'd suggest that whatever parts of the code have multithreaded access to the same `Vector`/`CowData` instance should be fixed to be mutex protected, to eliminate the risk of crash altogether.

Edit: The diff wouldn't actually work i think, but it should get the idea across.

```diff
index 75d0d346f4..d0ec6a0fbb 100644
--- a/core/templates/cowdata.h
+++ b/core/templates/cowdata.h
@@ -152,9 +152,13 @@ public:
                        return;
                }
 
-               _unref();
+               // Create a CowData to take over ownership of our ptr.
+               // This allows us to swap our ptr with some semblance of atomicity.
+               // While this doesn't make us fully atomic, it does decrease the chances
+               // of crashing under incorrect multithreaded access, at no extra cost.
+               CowData prev_data;
+               prev_data._ptr = _ptr;
                _ptr = p_from._ptr;
-               p_from._ptr = nullptr;
        }
 
        _FORCE_INLINE_ T *ptrw() {
@@ -550,13 +554,18 @@ void CowData<T>::_ref(const CowData &p_from) {
                return; // self assign, do nothing.
        }
 
-       _unref(); // Resets _ptr to nullptr.
-
        if (!p_from._ptr) {
-               return; //nothing to do
+               _unref();
+               return;
        }
 
-       if (p_from._get_refcount()->conditional_increment() > 0) { // could reference
+       if (p_from._get_refcount()->conditional_increment() > 0) {
+               // Create a CowData to take over ownership of our ptr.
+               // This allows us to swap our ptr with some semblance of atomicity.
+               // While this doesn't make us fully atomic, it does decrease the chances
+               // of crashing under incorrect multithreaded access, at no extra cost.
+               CowData prev_data;
+               prev_data._ptr = _ptr;
                _ptr = p_from._ptr;
        }
 }
```

> > Note that even with your fix, the change is not fully atomic. While it does significantly decrease the chance of crash, by removing the timeframe where _ptr is nullptr, there is still a chance of simultaneous read-write on _ptr. So, there's still chance of a crash.
> 
> I'm _definitely_ not going to pretend I'm 100% certain I've covered off everything. However, I can't see this. Are you able to elaborate?
> 
> The loop I've introduced in `_ref` is specifically designed to cover this. But it's certainly possible I've missed something.

Sure, I'll try to explain as I understand the situation.

Value writes to RAM themselves are not _guaranteed_ to be atomic. It's possible that, while one value is writing to `_ptr`, the other is reading from it, resulting in a read that is partially the old value, and partially the new one.

However, this information comes with the caveat that, on most modern architectures, the implementation _does_ make writes atomic at some size. On my computer, this seems to be 16 byte, demonstrated by the following code succeeding for `SIZE=16`, but failing for `SIZE=32`:

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <chrono>

bool running = true;

constexpr char SIZE = 32;

struct Value {
    char vs[SIZE] = {};
};

Value shared_value;

void writerThread() {
    while (running) {
        Value v = shared_value;
        for (size_t i = 0; i < SIZE; i++) {
            v.vs[i] = ~v.vs[i];
        }
        shared_value = v;
    }
}

void readerThread() {
    while (running) {
        Value value = shared_value;

        char ref = value.vs[0];
        for (size_t i = 1; i < SIZE; i++) {
            if (value.vs[i] != ref) {
                std::cout << "ref: " << value.vs[0] << "v: " << value.vs[i] << std::endl;
            }
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    std::thread writer(writerThread);
    std::thread reader(readerThread);

    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(10000));
    running = false;

    writer.join();
    reader.join();

    return 0;
}
```

Still, the atomicity is, I believe, not fully guaranteed, and thus cannot be relied on. The only way to have truly atomic operations is to use atomics, or threading primitives like Mutex, both of which come with significant performance cost.
